105
00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.960
Jenna, I even remember one day when he was on set that we quoted a line to him.

106
00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:22.200
We were like, do you remember the line where you and I were like, 13, 13!

107
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:22.940
Oh, yeah.

108
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.500
That was such a good scene.

109
00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.240
And he looked at us and he was like, okay, okay, ladies.

110
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:33.780
Well, if you could go back to reception, we're going to be like, but anyway, you guys, Rome

111
00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:34.780
was a show on HBO.

112
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.820
And in my opinion, Rome was the first Game of Thrones.

113
00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.640
It was very Game of Thrones-y.

114
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:41.940
Yes.

115
00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.280
It's extremely violent and bloody.

116
00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.540
And there's lots of warring political factions.

117
00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:49.880
What are you wearing?

118
00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:56.680
I'm wearing my Game of Thrones shirt because, because I don't know, Rome makes me think of

119
00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:57.240
Game of Thrones.

120
00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.880
So today on the podcast, I'm wearing my Game of Thrones shirt.

121
00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.480
Well, Julian also directed the first three seasons of Entourage, including Rainn Wilson's

122
00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.860
episode, I Love You Too.

123
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.200
Oh, I just think it's very funny because Charles McDougal, our other director, had also directed

124
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.240
episodes of Rome.

125
00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.820
I just think it's really funny that we had these guys who had been directing bloody battles

126
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.440
come into our little paper company, but they were so fun.

127
00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:31.980
I mean, yeah, the scale of production that they had to do with chariots tipping over and

128
00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:38.100
people flying through the air and then literally it'd be like, hey, can you use the stapler

129
00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.980
slightly harder next to the copier?

130
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:47.500
Like the difference in like our quote unquote action sequence on Dunder Mifflin versus Rome.

131
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.440
Well, I think part of Greg's thinking here was remembering that our show was not just a

132
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.360
comedy, that it had dramatic elements and was about real human people.

133
00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.440
And so by having some of these directors who are known for doing these dramas, these character

134
00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.560
driven stories, I think that might've been why he was drawn to them.

135
00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.700
I'm speculating, but that feels right.

136
00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.860
I like your speculation.

137
00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:10.800
I like it.

138
00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:11.900
Thank you.

139
00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:12.540
Okay.

